WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.800
<v Narrator>Next, let's talk about Kinesis video streams</v>

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.090
and it's integration with recognition.

3
00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.950
So, you are going to get one Kinesis video stream

4
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.950
per streaming device

5
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.000
and these devices are called producers.

6
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.480
So, a streaming device can be anything.

7
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.210
It can be a security camera, a body worn camera,

8
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.360
a smart phone or you can use something

9
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.630
called a Kineses Video Streams Producer Library

10
00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.000
to produce video streams.

11
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.859
But the idea is that you would create one video stream

12
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:24.750
for each streaming device.

13
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.707
So, if you are an IoT company and you're selling cameras

14
00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:29.660
if you have 1,000 cameras,

15
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.660
you need 1,000 video streams.

16
00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.840
The underlying data is going to be stored in S3

17
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.410
but we don't have access to it.

18
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:39.080
And you cannot output the stream data directly into S3.

19
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.190
You will need to consume the video stream

20
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.510
and build a custom solution to send the data into S3.

21
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.150
This is something that the exam will try to trick you on.

22
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.190
In terms of consumers for the video stream

23
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:51.530
it can be consumed by EC2 instances

24
00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:53.630
if you wanted to do some real time analysis

25
00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.730
or in batch or even send this to Amazonas 3.

26
00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.480
You can leverage the Kinesis Video Stream Parser Library

27
00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.150
to read this Kinesis Video Streams

28
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.710
or you can have an integration with AWS Rekognition

29
00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.840
to do some facial detection in your videos.

30
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.370
And this is the architecture I want to show you right now.

31
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.293
So we are going to get video producers

32
00:01:14.293 --> 00:01:18.340
and they will send data into a Kinesis Video Stream.

33
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.410
As I said, you can have a fleet of EC2 instances

34
00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.370
to read that video stream and do whatever you want with it.

35
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.480
Or you can have that stream go through Rekognition

36
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.540
and Rekognition can use

37
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.230
it's Internal Rekognition Face Collection

38
00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:34.500
to send some metadata as a new stream.

39
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.010
So you're going to create a Kinesis Data Stream out

40
00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.890
of Rekognition that will contain all the results

41
00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.380
of the analysis of the metadata of the video stream.

42
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.040
And from Kinesis Data Streams then we know how to use it.

43
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.720
We can use EC2 with the KCL library

44
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.630
or we can use Kinesis Data Firehose

45
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.380
or Kinesis Data Analytics.

46
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.410
And this opens up a lot of different architectures.

47
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.080
So, going into exam, remember that you

48
00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.710
can use Kinesis Video Streams in combination

49
00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.210
with Rekognition to extract some metadata out

50
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.479
of your stream and compare that against a database

51
00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:08.320
of face collection that's going to be internal

52
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.460
to rekognition.

53
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:10.293
That's it.

54
00:02:10.293 --> 00:02:11.900
I will see you in the next lecture.

